WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.459
The ground under Chennai feels fixed.

00:02.459 --> 00:06.492
It is not. It is moving toward the northeast at about five centimeters

00:06.492 --> 00:09.446
a year, a little faster than your fingernails grow.

00:09.446 --> 00:11.150
Chennai is riding on a plate.

00:11.150 --> 00:14.673
To see what that means, we need to look inside the Earth, and

00:14.673 --> 00:18.081
then at the edges of the pieces its surface is broken into.

00:18.081 --> 00:19.726
Earth has four main layers.

00:19.726 --> 00:22.431
The crust is the thin, solid outer layer.

00:22.431 --> 00:26.222
It is about five kilometers thick under the oceans, and up to

00:26.222 --> 00:28.641
about seventy under the highest mountains.

00:28.641 --> 00:30.218
Below it is the mantle.

00:30.218 --> 00:34.640
The mantle is hot, solid rock, but over millions of years it flows

00:34.640 --> 00:37.454
very slowly, like extremely stiff putty.

00:37.454 --> 00:41.161
It goes down to two thousand nine hundred kilometers.

00:41.161 --> 00:45.040
Below that is the outer core, which is liquid iron and nickel.

00:45.040 --> 00:47.161
At the center is the inner core.

00:47.161 --> 00:50.561
It is even hotter, but it is solid, because the pressure there

00:50.561 --> 00:54.801
is enormous. Heat from deep inside keeps the mantle slowly moving,

00:54.801 --> 00:57.325
and that drives everything else in this unit.

00:57.325 --> 00:58.807
A plate is not just crust.

00:58.807 --> 01:02.539
The crust and the rigid top part of the mantle are locked together,

01:02.539 --> 01:05.063
and together they are called the lithosphere.

01:05.063 --> 01:07.368
It is about one hundred kilometers thick.

01:07.368 --> 01:11.154
Underneath is a weaker layer of the mantle called the asthenosphere,

01:11.154 --> 01:14.831
which can flow. The lithosphere is broken into about a dozen large

01:14.831 --> 01:16.477
plates and many smaller ones.

01:16.477 --> 01:19.934
They slide over the asthenosphere at a few centimeters a year.

01:19.934 --> 01:22.130
The middle of a plate is usually quiet.

01:22.130 --> 01:24.928
The action is at the edges, where two plates meet.

01:24.928 --> 01:28.550
That is where you find most earthquakes, most volcanoes, and most

01:28.550 --> 01:32.048
mountain ranges. There are three ways two plates can meet.

01:32.048 --> 01:34.746
At a divergent boundary, the plates move apart.

01:34.746 --> 01:37.203
At a convergent boundary, they move together.

01:37.203 --> 01:40.130
At a transform boundary, they slide past each other.

01:40.130 --> 01:43.260
Each one makes different landforms and different hazards, so we

01:43.260 --> 01:44.603
will take them one at a time.

01:44.603 --> 01:47.310
At a divergent boundary the plates move apart.

01:47.310 --> 01:50.074
Hot mantle rises into the gap and partly melts.

01:50.074 --> 01:52.320
The magma cools and becomes new crust.

01:52.320 --> 01:56.006
Most divergent boundaries are under the oceans, where they form

01:56.006 --> 01:59.288
long underwater mountain chains called mid-ocean ridges.

01:59.288 --> 02:02.974
The Mid-Atlantic Ridge is one, and Iceland sits right on top of

02:02.974 --> 02:06.947
it. When a divergent boundary forms on land, it makes a rift valley,

02:06.947 --> 02:08.560
like the East African Rift.

02:08.560 --> 02:12.246
The hazards here are frequent but mostly small earthquakes, and

02:12.246 --> 02:15.701
eruptions of runny lava that tends to flow and not explode.

02:15.701 --> 02:18.638
At a convergent boundary the plates move together.

02:18.638 --> 02:22.439
When an ocean plate meets a continent, the ocean plate is denser,

02:22.439 --> 02:23.821
so it sinks underneath.

02:23.821 --> 02:25.376
This is called subduction.

02:25.376 --> 02:28.140
A deep trench forms where the plate bends down.

02:28.140 --> 02:30.789
A line of volcanoes forms on the plate above.

02:30.789 --> 02:33.668
And the largest earthquakes on Earth happen here.

02:33.668 --> 02:35.396
There are two other versions.

02:35.396 --> 02:39.082
When an ocean plate meets another ocean plate, one still sinks,

02:39.082 --> 02:42.652
and the volcanoes build a curved chain of islands, like Japan

02:42.652 --> 02:46.108
and Indonesia. When two continents meet, neither one sinks.

02:46.108 --> 02:48.526
The crust crumples upward into mountains.

02:48.526 --> 02:51.982
That is how the Himalaya formed, as India pushed into Asia.

02:51.982 --> 02:55.322
At a transform boundary the plates slide past each other.

02:55.322 --> 02:59.353
Crust is not made and it is not destroyed, so there are no volcanoes.

02:59.353 --> 03:01.541
But the plates do not slide smoothly.

03:01.541 --> 03:05.227
They stick, and then they slip, and each slip is an earthquake.

03:05.227 --> 03:09.143
These earthquakes are shallow, and they are often damaging, because

03:09.143 --> 03:11.447
the faults can run right beside cities.

03:11.447 --> 03:14.960
The San Andreas Fault in California is a transform boundary.

03:14.960 --> 03:17.436
So is the North Anatolian Fault in Turkey.

03:17.436 --> 03:19.970
One setting is not a plate boundary at all.

03:19.970 --> 03:23.598
A hotspot is a place where a plume of hot rock rises from deep

03:23.598 --> 03:27.168
in the mantle. The plume stays in roughly one place while the

03:27.168 --> 03:28.378
plate moves over it.

03:28.378 --> 03:29.587
It builds a volcano.

03:29.587 --> 03:33.158
The plate carries that volcano away, and a new one forms over

03:33.158 --> 03:36.958
the plume. The result is a chain of volcanoes, with the youngest,

03:36.958 --> 03:40.644
active one at one end, and older, worn-down ones farther along.

03:40.644 --> 03:42.429
Hawaii is the classic example.

03:42.429 --> 03:44.330
So where is Chennai in all this?

03:44.330 --> 03:47.958
Chennai sits near the middle of the Indian plate, which is why

03:47.958 --> 03:49.858
large earthquakes are rare here.

03:49.858 --> 03:52.910
But two plate boundaries are close enough to matter.

03:52.910 --> 03:55.444
To the north, India is colliding with Asia.

03:55.444 --> 03:59.303
That collision built the Himalaya, and it causes large earthquakes

03:59.303 --> 04:00.800
in Nepal and North India.

04:00.800 --> 04:04.428
To the east, across the Bay of Bengal, the plate sinks beneath

04:04.428 --> 04:08.229
Southeast Asia, along a trench that runs past the Andaman Islands

04:08.229 --> 04:12.087
and Sumatra. In December two thousand four, that boundary slipped.

04:12.087 --> 04:16.003
The tsunami it made crossed the bay and reached Chennai in a little

04:16.003 --> 04:19.056
over two hours. Check yourself with three questions.

04:19.056 --> 04:20.323
Pause if you need to.

04:20.323 --> 04:22.856
One. Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core.

04:22.856 --> 04:24.181
Which layer is liquid?

04:24.181 --> 04:26.427
Two. Two plates slide past each other.

04:26.427 --> 04:29.422
Which boundary is that, and does it have volcanoes?

04:29.422 --> 04:31.207
Three. Two continents collide.

04:31.207 --> 04:33.165
What forms? Here are the answers.

04:33.165 --> 04:35.238
The outer core is the liquid layer.

04:35.238 --> 04:38.866
Plates sliding past each other make a transform boundary, with

04:38.866 --> 04:40.594
earthquakes but no volcanoes.

04:40.594 --> 04:44.164
And colliding continents build mountains, because neither one

04:44.164 --> 04:46.583
sinks. Now make your four settings table.
